          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Throughout
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            there are various entities referred to as “numbers”; in modern mathematics it would be more accurate to refer to any one of various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            called “number systems”, and simply define a
            %em number
            to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
            of such a type.
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numeral"} numeral
            on the other hand is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntactic
            representation of a number, part of a system of numeration.
          %p
            It is interesting to try to describe the general conditions under which something comes to be designated as a “number”. After all, such number systems tend to form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+rings"} commutative rings
            or at least commutative
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigs"} rigs
          %p
            The root notion, known to the great ancient civilizations and particularly ancient Hellenic civilization, is that of (the system of)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
          %ul
            %li
              systems of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfinite+arithmetic"} transfinite numbers
              (e.g., cardinal numbers, ordinal numbers, surreal numbers)
          %p
            Roughly speaking, transfinite numbers tend to be quantities that measure sizes of elements in ZF-type structures (as in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+set+theory"} algebraic set theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} decategorification
          %ul
            %li
              structures arising in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+number+theory"} algebraic number theory
          %p In algebraic number theory, there seem to be two distinct general trends in the creation of new number systems from old (starting from the natural numbers):
          %ul
            %li
              %ul
                %li
                  Passing to a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completion"} completion
                  in some sense
            %li
              %ul
                %li
                  Passing to a
                  %em finite
                  extension of some sort
          %p
            For example, one passes from the rig of natural numbers to the ring of integers through additive
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+completion"} group completion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+domain"} integral domain
            of integers to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            of rationals through a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+of+fractions"} field of fractions
            completion. Or, one constructs number fields as finite extensions of the field of rational numbers (similarly, algebraic integers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            inside such a number field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K/\mathbb{Q}
            as those elements such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[\alpha]
            is a finite rank extension of
            = succeed ")." do
